  "query_preview": "..\nThis Afternoon through Thursday night: An upper-level trough of\nlower pressure will be pushing onshore and displacing a ridge \nof higher pressure over the Inland Northwest to the east. Weak \nmid-level instability across the northern zones has resulted in \na low of altocumulus castellanus (ACCAS) clouds, and a few of \nthese may develop into deeper convective cells with isolated \nthunderstorms through the rest of the afternoon today. \nOtherwise, it\u2019s a hot day and the Heat Advisory of the Lewiston-\nClarkston Valley remains in effect until 8PM this evening. \nFires in the Cascades are expected to become more active later \nthis afternoon and westerly winds is expected to push this smoke\nacross the Columbia Basin tonight into the Spokane/Coeur \nd\u2019Alene Area by Thursday morning. A lot of uncertainty though \nwith how impactful this smoke will be as far as decreasing our \nair quality.\n\nA cold front pass for tomorrow increases our winds. This will \nhelp to clear out smoke, but it will also present fire weather \nconcerns with relative humidity values near to or at critical \nthresholds. The best chances for seeing red flag conditions with\nsustained winds greater than 20 mph and relative humidity \nlowering to around 20% will be across the Waterville Plateau and\nup the Okanogan Valley. The Fire Weather Watch will be upgraded\nto a Red Flag Warning in effect for Thursday afternoon into the\nearly evening. Any new fires that get started will have the \npotential for spread rapidly. Winds are expected to be sustained\nat 15-25 mph with gusts of around 30-35 mph. Temperatures \ntomorrow will be much cooler at 8-12 degrees cooler than today.\n\nFriday through Sunday night: This period will be the start of a\npattern shift with the Four Corners High Pressure system\nremaining suppressed and longwave troughing of lower pressure\ntaking hold over the Inland Northwest. This brings high\nconfidence for below normal temperatures heading through the\nweekend with highs expected to drop down into the 70s. A closed\nupper-level low pressure area will migrate over the region for\nSaturday and this will give us a shot at showers, primarily over\nthe northern mountains. There will be a risk for afternoon\nthunderstorms as well, but storms are expected to contain\nrainfall with them and not as much of a concern for fire weather\nconcerns if expected to be drier. Rain with storms will have\nopportunity to be wetting, but looks to just be contained under\nthe cores of storms and unlikely to be widespread wetting rains.\n\nMonday through Wednesday: Shortwave ridging of higher pressure\nfor Monday will bring a temporary bump up in our temperatures\nwith highs warming back up into the 80s. Then another low\npressure trough will push across for Tuesday. This will drop\ntemperatures below normal and increase chances for showers by\nWednesday. /SVH",
